\title{Declarative Logger Configuration}
\description{
lgr can be configured from a single canonical, versioned configuration
format (\code{version: 1}) that can express the \emph{entire} logger hierarchy: the
root logger, descendant loggers, and reusable named \code{layouts}, \code{filters}
and \code{appenders}. Use \link[=Logger]{Logger$config()} (usually via \code{lgr$config()})
to apply such a config, or \code{\link[=read_config]{read_config()}}/\code{\link[=validate_config]{validate_config()}} to check a
config without applying it.
}
\section{Top-level structure}{

A config document is a mapping with an optional \code{version} (defaults to
\code{1}; no other value is currently supported), an optional
\code{disable_existing_loggers} flag, and the optional sections \code{layouts},
\code{filters}, \code{appenders} and \code{loggers}. \code{loggers} is a mapping from logger
name (\code{root} for the root logger, \code{/}-separated names such as \code{app/db} for
descendants) to a logger definition supporting \code{class} (\code{"Logger"} or
\code{"LoggerGlue"}), \code{threshold}, \code{appenders}, \code{filters}, \code{propagate} and
\code{exception_handler}. \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/\code{appenders} are mappings from a
user-chosen component name to a component definition: a mapping with a
\code{class} key (e.g. \code{class: AppenderConsole}, optionally namespaced as
\code{pkg::ClassName}) plus the remaining keys as constructor arguments.
Component names can be referenced by name wherever a \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/
\code{appenders} value is expected (in another component, or in a logger); such
references are resolved once and the resulting object is shared. Appenders
and filters can also be defined inline instead of referenced by name.

Function-valued positions -- a logger's \code{exception_handler}, filter
entries, and (as of the generalized form) \strong{any} constructor argument of
a \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/\code{appenders} component (e.g. a \code{LayoutJson}'s
\code{transform_event}, or an appender's dynamic \code{index}) -- accept one of: a
\strong{name reference} (a bare \code{character} scalar for \code{exception_handler}, or
an explicit \verb{\{ ref: <name> \}}), resolved by lookup (\verb{<pkg>::<name>}
supported); an \strong{inline code} form (\code{{ code: "<R source>" }}), the only
form that evaluates config-supplied source and is therefore rejected
unless the loader is called with \code{allow_code = TRUE}; and, for filters
only, a \verb{\{ class: <FilterClass>, ... \}} spec (constructing an R6
\link{EventFilter}) or a bare-string reference to a named \code{filters} component.
Outside of these explicit \code{{ ref: ... }}/\code{{ code: ... }} forms, a plain
\code{character} scalar in an ordinary argument position is always a literal
string, never an implicit function reference.
}

\section{\code{enabled}}{

Component definitions under \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/\code{appenders}, and logger
definitions under \code{loggers}, accept an optional \code{enabled} key (a boolean,
defaulting to \code{TRUE}) -- the natural target for \verb{$\{VAR:-false\}}
interpolation, so a sink can be toggled per environment without adding or
removing YAML blocks. When \code{enabled: false} on a \emph{named} component, it is
not instantiated, and any \verb{appenders:}/\verb{filters:} list entry elsewhere
that references it by name is silently dropped rather than treated as an
"unknown reference" error; an inline (unnamed) component definition with
\code{enabled: false} is likewise dropped from whichever list it appears in.
When \code{enabled: false} on a logger definition, that logger is fully reset
to its default/unconfigured state (as if it had never been configured),
regardless of whatever state it was in before -- this keeps re-applying
the config idempotent, rather than depending on the logger's prior state.
}

\section{Applying a config is a full snapshot, not a patch}{

A logger definition under \code{loggers} fully determines that logger's
\code{threshold}, \code{appenders}, \code{filters}, \code{propagate} and \code{exception_handler}:
any of these keys that are \emph{not} given for a listed logger are reset to
their default (no threshold/inherited, no appenders, no filters,
\code{propagate: true}, the default exception handler) rather than left as they
were before the config was applied. This makes re-applying the same
declarative config (or switching between config files) reproducible and
independent of whatever state a Logger happened to be in beforehand. Note
that this differs from the plain (non-declarative) \code{list} form accepted by
\link[=Logger]{Logger$config()}, which only touches the elements it is given and
leaves everything else untouched.
}

\section{\code{disable_existing_loggers}}{

If \code{TRUE}, every pre-existing non-root logger \emph{not} named under \code{loggers}
is reset to its default/unconfigured state before the rest of the config
is applied (loggers that \emph{are} named under \code{loggers} are unaffected by
this flag; they always end up in the state the config describes for them,
per the previous section). Defaults to \code{FALSE}, so orphaned loggers are
left alone unless you opt in.

\strong{Resetting is not the same as silencing.} A reset logger gets
\code{propagate = TRUE} and no appenders/threshold of its own, i.e. it now
inherits its ancestors' appenders and threshold. If a logger was
deliberately muted (e.g. \code{propagate: false} with no appenders) before the
reset, this can make it \emph{more} verbose afterwards, not less -- it starts
forwarding events to whichever ancestor appenders it now inherits from.
}

\section{Environment variable interpolation}{

Scalar string values may contain \verb{$\{VAR\}} (substituted with the value of
environment variable \code{VAR}) or \verb{$\{VAR:-default\}} (using \code{default} when
\code{VAR} is unset or empty). A literal \verb{$\{...\}} can be produced with the
escape \verb{$$\{...\}}. A value produced \emph{entirely} by interpolation is
leniently coerced (number-like to \code{numeric}, \code{true}/\code{false} to \code{logical}),
so \verb{size: $\{SIZE\}} behaves like a literal \code{size: 1048576}. Interpolation
happens before validation and is never applied to \verb{code:} payloads.
}

\section{Safety}{

Loading a config never evaluates config-supplied R source by default.
Only the \verb{code:} form does so, and it is rejected during validation
unless the caller passes \code{allow_code = TRUE}. All other forms (name/\code{ref}
references, \code{class} specs, named-component references) are data, not
source, and are always available.
}

\section{Environment-layered configs}{

lgr's config format itself is deliberately \emph{not} environment-aware -- it
describes one logger hierarchy, not a \code{default}/\code{qa}/\code{prod} bundle of
them. If you need different values per deployment stage, merge them
\emph{before} calling \code{\link[=read_config]{read_config()}}/\link[=Logger]{Logger$config()}, rather than
asking lgr to do the merging:
\itemize{
\item for simple cases, \verb{$\{VAR\}}/\verb{$\{VAR:-default\}} interpolation (see above)
is usually enough on its own -- e.g. \verb{isEnabled: $\{ELASTIC_ENABLED:-false\}};
\item for config files that look like:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{default:
  logger:
    appenders: \{...\}
qa:
  logger:
    appenders: \{...\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

(one \code{default} section plus per-environment overrides, a common pattern
e.g. with the \pkg{config} package), load and merge the relevant
sections yourself -- \code{config::get(file = "logger.yaml", config = "qa")}
or a plain \code{utils::modifyList(default_cfg, env_cfg)} -- and pass the
\emph{already-merged} \code{list} to \code{read_config()}/\code{Logger$config()}.
}
}

